WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.210
Our school bus leaves at six every

2
00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:05.430
morning and visits nine villages.

3
00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:07.760
The children from the first

4
00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:10.040
village ride for over an hour.

5
00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:12.330
Is there a shorter route,

6
00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:13.530
and a fairer one?

7
00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:16.700
Today: the school bus route,

8
00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:19.080
built fast and then improved.

9
00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:23.390
Visit every village once and come back,

10
00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:25.950
by the shortest route: that’s the

11
00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:27.470
travelling salesperson problem.

12
00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:29.860
It sounds easy, but the

13
00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:31.440
routes multiply fast.

14
00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:34.030
Nine villages have a hundred and

15
00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:35.630
eighty-one thousand routes.

16
00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:39.490
Twenty have more than a billion billion.

17
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.020
A quick rule: from wherever the

18
00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:45.240
bus is, drive to the closest

19
00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:47.120
village it hasn’t visited yet.

20
00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:49.690
It’s fast, and usually decent.

21
00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:53.180
But it never looks ahead, so its

22
00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:55.720
last legs home are often long.

23
00:00:57.010 --> 00:00:59.190
In Python, it’s a few lines.

24
00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:01.310
Keep a list for the route.

25
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.280
While villages are left, add the

26
00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:05.920
one closest to the last stop.

27
00:01:07.070 --> 00:01:08.130
Then improve it.

28
00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:11.290
2-opt takes out two roads, and

29
00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:12.870
puts the stretch between them

30
00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:14.290
back the other way round.

31
00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:17.590
If the route got shorter, keep it, and

32
00:01:17.590 --> 00:01:19.850
try again, until no swap helps.

33
00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:23.380
In the lab, nearest neighbour drives

34
00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:25.000
thirty-four point six kilometres.

35
00:01:25.790 --> 00:01:28.170
Three 2-opt swaps bring it down to

36
00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:30.850
twenty-eight point six, the best route

37
00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:31.370
there is.

38
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.060
That’s six kilometres saved,

39
00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:35.580
every single morning.

40
00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:37.320
Quick check!

41
00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:40.660
2-opt has stopped: no swap of two

42
00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:41.860
roads makes the route shorter.

43
00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:44.780
Is it now the best route of all?

44
00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:46.620
Not always.

45
00:01:47.410 --> 00:01:50.630
On some maps, 2-opt gets stuck: no

46
00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:53.010
single swap helps, but the best

47
00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:54.110
route is still shorter.

48
00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:57.370
For a few villages, a computer can

49
00:01:57.370 --> 00:01:59.430
check every route and be sure.

50
00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:02.880
For many, real route planners build a

51
00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:05.400
quick route and improve it, just like

52
00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:05.680
this.

53
00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:08.580
Length isn’t the only measure:

54
00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:11.220
the children picked up first ride

55
00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:12.240
all the way to school.

56
00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:14.940
On the small map in today’s code,

57
00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:16.900
nearest neighbour drives

58
00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:19.140
twenty-three kilometres, and the

59
00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:21.440
first village rides twenty-one of them.

60
00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:24.240
The best route is nineteen, with

61
00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:25.660
a longest ride of sixteen.

62
00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:28.380
Judge a route twice: the

63
00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:30.400
total, and the longest ride.

64
00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:34.170
In the app, open “The school bus route”.

65
00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:36.830
Press Run, and watch nearest

66
00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:39.510
neighbour, then the 2-opt swaps.

67
00:02:40.130 --> 00:02:42.470
Press “Find the best route” to compare.

68
00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:46.150
Then try a new map: does 2-opt

69
00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:47.390
always reach the best?

70
00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:49.150
Your turn.

71
00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:51.800
Route A is two kilometres

72
00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:54.080
shorter, but one village’s

73
00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:55.800
children ride for fifty minutes.

74
00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:58.580
On route B, nobody rides

75
00:02:58.580 --> 00:02:59.660
more than thirty-five.

76
00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:01.090
How should we choose?

77
00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:04.680
Agree a longest-ride limit with

78
00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:06.600
the families, then find the

79
00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:08.140
shortest route that keeps it.

80
00:03:08.690 --> 00:03:10.990
Remember: routes multiply too

81
00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:12.350
fast to try them all.

82
00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:14.450
Nearest neighbour builds a

83
00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:17.110
route, and 2-opt improves it.

84
00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:19.740
And judge a route twice: the

85
00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:21.740
total, and the longest ride.

86
00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:25.100
Try it in class tomorrow: pupils

87
00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:26.880
stand as villages around the

88
00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:29.240
playground, and one pupil is the bus.

89
00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:32.640
Drive nearest neighbour, counting steps.

90
00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:35.460
Then find two paths that cross,

91
00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:37.660
